Samuele Marro: Oh, okay.

45
00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:26.030
Samuele Marro: So, I'm Sam, I'm currently doing a PhD in Oxford. I work with Microsoft on agent communication and collaboration.

46
00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:31.009
Samuele Marro: And besides that, I'm the chair of the Live28 Agent Standards Working Group.

47
00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.919
Samuele Marro: And so today, I wanted to give, again, I've met Eric

48
00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:43.630
Samuele Marro: through this working group. And so I wanted to give an overview of what we do, and what we're focusing on right now, which is, in particular, agent permissions.json.

49
00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.489
Samuele Marro: So, in short.

50
00:08:46.870 --> 00:09:02.700
Samuele Marro: The last WG was formed after two conversations I had. One was with Stanford… the Stanford HAEI, which is the Human AI Interaction Institute, where we basically were discussing that we need standards for LLM agents.

51
00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:04.859
Samuele Marro: And nobody…

52
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.199
Samuele Marro: Or at least at the moment, at the time when we were discussing it, there was nobody building it.

53
00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:17.780
Samuele Marro: And so I went to speak with the W3 folks, because I work a lot with some folks connected to the team.

54
00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:19.030
Samuele Marro: And,

55
00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:32.240
Samuele Marro: we… basically, they told me, yes, we're aware that there are, you know, LM agents being created, and they need standards, but we're not gonna… we're not gonna codify anything because the industry is not mature enough.

56
00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:41.690
Samuele Marro: And so this creates a sort of, like, chicken egg scenario, where first you wanna… you need some maturity, but in order to get maturity, you need the infrastructure, and so on.

57
00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:54.980
Samuele Marro: And so, this was how the last ABG was formed. The idea is we create simple standards that are not meant to be the final standards that will be used for… in the future.

58
00:09:55.210 --> 00:10:01.059
Samuele Marro: That's the… that's the job at W4C, ITF, all of… all the various standard organizations.

59
00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:07.360
Samuele Marro: And by doing that, we push the system towards a level of maturity, when then the official

60
00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:12.790
Samuele Marro: organizations can come and standardize. So we mostly focus on

61
00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:17.480
Samuele Marro: Simple standards, fast situation, and these sorts of things.

62
00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:28.000
Samuele Marro: And in practice, it's mostly… the members are mostly a mix of agent companies and universities with labs working on agents.

63
00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:33.169
Samuele Marro: with a couple extra additions, such as the Cosmos Institute, for example, which is a non-profit.

64
00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:39.389
Samuele Marro: And, yeah, so we meet, by week, fourth and nightly, and,

65
00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.620
Samuele Marro: We mostly… we've been mostly working right now on the permission side of agents.

66
00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:49.699
Samuele Marro: And in particular, we're doing some work on agentpermissions.json, we call it.

67
00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.650
Samuele Marro: Basically, the… The problem is that.

68
00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:58.749
Samuele Marro: Right now, when you have a Belser agent.

69
00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:15.090
Samuele Marro: you do not have any system with which you can say what you can and cannot do as an agent, in the sense of a browser using LLM-based agent when interacting with a website. We have robots.60,

70
00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:20.630
Samuele Marro: But in practice, a lot of the… of the side of standards.

71
00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:25.210
Samuele Marro: surrounding interactions between agents and UIs is not there.

72
00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:33.530
Samuele Marro: And, you know, impact is what's up… what's happened is that the… there's been, an increase in the use of,

73
00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:46.470
Samuele Marro: of 50s, there's been, the most obvious example of pushback against this practice has been Cloudflare's AI Labient, which I don't know if you're familiar, but it's basically,

74
00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:47.250
Neil Thomson (QueryVision): That's neutral.

75
00:11:47.580 --> 00:12:03.170
Samuele Marro: AI-generated garbage that is fed to the web page, so that if the… if the agent, like, if the website thinks that it's interacting with an agent, then it shows some garbage that a normal human will never read.

76
00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:07.839
Samuele Marro: But in fact, it keeps it locked in a sort of loop, exploring more and more pages.

77
00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:09.410
Samuele Marro: They go nowhere.

78
00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:14.080
Samuele Marro: In practice, this is very funny and very smart.

79
00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:23.550
Samuele Marro: But you're blocking all the legitimate use… uses of bursa agents. Stuff like accessibility tools, stuff like,

80
00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:33.880
Samuele Marro: gathering data for pulling different data from sources. Stuff like, in general, executing workflows on behalf of a user.

81
00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:41.389
Samuele Marro: All of these… Can be very useful for a human, but they're blocked, because the… right now, the

82
00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.579
Samuele Marro: The websites tend to block every form of agent interaction.

83
00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.749
Samuele Marro: And, in a way.

84
00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:55.000
Samuele Marro: This was quite similar to what was happening with robots.txt.txt back then, where you had indiscriminate curling.

85
00:12:55.290 --> 00:13:02.990
Samuele Marro: And then you had some legitimate query uses, and websites could not Tell it apart.

86
00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:10.649
Samuele Marro: In practice, how did they fix this? They did it with robots.txt. And so that's what we've been doing, essentially.

87
00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.730
Samuele Marro: The agentpermissions.json is a simple robots.txt-style document.

88
00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:26.409
Samuele Marro: Where we have interactions with a web page, and we specify what can and cannot be done when an agent interacts with a website.

89
00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.840
Samuele Marro: There's 3 main forms of guidance.

90
00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:42.659
Samuele Marro: One is on the type of resources that can be accessed. For example, you can say, you must not click this button, you must not read the contents of this, of this paragraph, etc.

91
00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:53.889
Samuele Marro: Some general actions that can be performed. These are much broader in the sense, so it's something like you must not message users, for example.

92
00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:56.569
Samuele Marro: And, when possible.

93
00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:14.070
Samuele Marro: we use… we also say, hey, agent, instead of interacting with my UI, call this API, use this MCP tool, and so you have to avoid… you avoid all of the… all of the UI interaction, and I don't spend a lot of time and bandwidth serving this UI to you.

94
00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:23.050
Samuele Marro: And… In practice, why have 3 different specifications in the same document?

95
00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:30.430
Samuele Marro: Because they… They handle very specific, alternatives for control of an agent.

96
00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:36.380
Samuele Marro: So, in the sense that, for the resource rules, you must not click this button.

97
00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:44.809
Samuele Marro: you can put it at the level of the browser. Like, a lot of agents use a headless browser to interact with a web page.

98
00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:53.580
Samuele Marro: You can… between the LLM, which performs the actions, and the… the actual browser, you can put some rules.

99
00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:55.610
Samuele Marro: And so, in practice, you can,

100
00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:01.450
Samuele Marro: You can enforce these rules, like, you must not click this button, in a very strict way.

101
00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:09.659
Samuele Marro: Instead, for stuff like, you must not be able to message users, which cannot be formalized, in that sense.

102
00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:17.940
Samuele Marro: we use it as part of the permit. So, in practice, the LM has access to the… to this information.

103
00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.410
Samuele Marro: But it's up to DLM to follow it.

104
00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.419
Samuele Marro: So in this way, it's not…

105
00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:30.329
Samuele Marro: as tightly controlled as it would be for the, for access to specific HTML elements.

106
00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:31.570
Samuele Marro: And finally.

107
00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:41.969
Samuele Marro: why you offer these alternative pathways? Because in practice, MCP tools will… and API routes will always be more reliable than interacting with,

108
00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:43.669
Samuele Marro: With a web page.

109
00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:48.180
Samuele Marro: And in practice, it's what the developer or the website wants.

110
00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:56.800
Samuele Marro: to use. Like, if you're an agent, ideally, whenever there's an API available, you should use that instead.

111
00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:06.290
Samuele Marro: Now, Like Robots.txt, They… this is not, there is no enforcement layer.

112
00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:12.439
Samuele Marro: And this is a very spec… like, it's a very… Intentional design decision.

113
00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:16.630
Samuele Marro: The idea is that, right now, the ecosystem is not

114
00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.030
Samuele Marro: powerful enough. And, like, there's not enough maturity

115
00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:31.219
Samuele Marro: to pick one specific ID system, and one specific permission system, and an enforcement system, and ask every agent to follow those rules or get locked out.

116
00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:34.870
Samuele Marro: This, in practice, is not reliable for…

117
00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:38.870
Samuele Marro: For the purpose of enforcing a standard.

118
00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:42.120
Samuele Marro: Instead, like robots.txty.

119
00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:48.630
Samuele Marro: We want to lay the groundwork for this type of stuff, where first we start by… by…

120
00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:55.130
Samuele Marro: Having a way for websites to say, hey, if you're a good agent, Follow these rules.

121
00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.240
Samuele Marro: That reduces the malicious traffic done by good agents.

122
00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:05.110
Samuele Marro: Obviously, it will not stop malicious agents from interacting with your website.

123
00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:11.560
Samuele Marro: But… It's fundamentally a problem of scale when we talk about agent interactions with a website.

124
00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:18.380
Samuele Marro: Agent interaction with your web page becomes a problem when you have too much of it.

125
00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:22.350
Samuele Marro: If you can cut down on the… on the…

126
00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:26.990
Samuele Marro: On the invalid interactions that you want to block.

127
00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:33.859
Samuele Marro: And you have… Some level, a certain percentage of agents that follow the rules.

128
00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:45.350
Samuele Marro: In practice, you're reducing the bandwidth problems, you're reducing the intensity of these interaction problems, and that's why, in practice, you can

129
00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:54.510
Samuele Marro: It forms a less adversary relationship between the agent companies and the websites.

130
00:17:55.100 --> 00:18:00.119
Samuele Marro: And to give you an example, I have here, like, an example of the…

131
00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:05.439
Samuele Marro: of the standard. Simple JSON file, We submit a data.

132
00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:10.570
Samuele Marro: So… And here we can see the example for resources.

133
00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:17.750
Samuele Marro: So, for example, You are always… for all HTML elements.

134
00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.850
Samuele Marro: You are allowed to eat content.

135
00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:30.199
Samuele Marro: But you're… you're forbidden from following links, that… that match this CSS.

136
00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:31.540
Samuele Marro: private area.

137
00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.990
Samuele Marro: and you… But you're allowed to click Elements.

138
00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:44.020
Samuele Marro: that match this specific XPath. So in practice, you can use either CSS selectors or XPath selectors.

139
00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:51.430
Samuele Marro: And in practice, there are also some modifiers you can do, where you can, for example, limit the number of interactions.

140
00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:53.810
Samuele Marro: In a set of time window.

141
00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.920
Samuele Marro: This is the… this is for resource rules.

142
00:18:58.110 --> 00:19:00.690
Samuele Marro: Instead, when we talk about… so…

143
00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.470
Samuele Marro: Instead, when we talk about action guidelines.

144
00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:12.500
Samuele Marro: You can have stuff like, you must not send direct messages to users, except blah blah blah blah.

145
00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:20.140
Samuele Marro: And finally, for the APIs, we basically… we keep it… we keep it very, very, very simple. It's just stuff like, hey.

146
00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:24.120
Samuele Marro: if… If you're sending a message to the user.

147
00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:32.480
Samuele Marro: How about instead you use this OpenAPI endpoint you can find here, and here you can find the…

148
00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:33.920
Samuele Marro: the documentation.

149
00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:47.440
Samuele Marro: And this is pretty much it. Like, there's a few extra things for some modifiers, but in practice, it's a JSON file that can be written in 5 minutes.

150
00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:53.069
Samuele Marro: And for mo… for the majority, Of, of uses.

151
00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:55.700
Samuele Marro: You can just leave these action guidelines.

152
00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:01.959
Samuele Marro: And say, hey, please, agent, don't do these specific things I don't like, and that's it.

153
00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:12.110
Samuele Marro: Or, if you really want to ban the user of agents at all, you can just do, selector all allowed false.

154
00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:15.600
Samuele Marro: And basically, you're forbidding all interactions with the website.

155
00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:20.270
Samuele Marro: And… Any questions about this stuff?

156
00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:26.659
Samuele Marro: Before… before we start, like, the proper discussion, etc.

157
00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:33.290
Wenjing Chu (Futurewei Technologies, Inc.): Are these rules, just recommendations, or…

158
00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:33.860
Samuele Marro: is…

159
00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:37.009
Wenjing Chu (Futurewei Technologies, Inc.): Their enforcement behind it.

160
00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:43.989
Samuele Marro: the recommendations in the style of Avast.TX team, as I explained, a bit before.

161
00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:54.400
Samuele Marro: We… right now, there is not enough maturity, enough consolidation in terms of agent interaction practices, which is why we

162
00:20:55.100 --> 00:21:04.160
Samuele Marro: The reasoning is that We cannot enforce all agent interactions, because fundamentally, An agent interaction is very…

163
00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:07.150
Samuele Marro: Hard to distinguish from a user interaction.

164
00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:14.639
Samuele Marro: What we do is that when there are legitimate interactions with… the legitimate agents interacting with a web page.

165
00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.500
Samuele Marro: Then, we can put some… some norms around that.

166
00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:23.219
Samuele Marro: Is this enough to prevent malicious uses? Obviously not.

167
00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:26.100
Samuele Marro: But it's more semi-scale.

168
00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:28.600
Samuele Marro: The objective of this standard is to allow.

169
00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:34.400
Samuele Marro: genuine, genuine agents, to interact.

170
00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:43.340
Samuele Marro: with a web page in the appropriate way, in the same way that robots.txtea allows genuine callers to interact with the website.

171
00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:46.130
Samuele Marro: Neither of them stop malicious use.

172
00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:51.939
Samuele Marro: But it's the scale that is the main problem, rather than the specific malicious use.

173
00:21:52.910 --> 00:22:00.850
Samuele Marro: And right now, again, since we have a lot of partners with, like, aging organizations, We're looking into…

174
00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:04.750
Samuele Marro: We test… we're working with, with them.

175
00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:06.739
Samuele Marro: to test how…

176
00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:20.389
Samuele Marro: how much burden an agent gets from following these extra permissions? Because, of course, this is extra information in the context of the language model, it's extra informa… it's extra rules surrounding the interaction with the web page.

177
00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:24.219
Samuele Marro: In… so we're working with them to make sure that

178
00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:27.000
Samuele Marro: It doesn't burden the agent too much.

179
00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:34.240
Samuele Marro: And in the meantime, we're talking with Cloudflare, because they've been also working a lot on gating interactions between

180
00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:36.230
Samuele Marro: Agents and websites.

181
00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:48.929
